# Loan Default Prediction | Python, Data Science & Machine Learning

Predicts whether a borrower will default on a loan from employment status, bank balance and annual salary. The project shows how an imbalanced target can make accuracy misleading, and how class weighting and better metrics fix that.

## Dataset

- 10,000 borrower records, no missing values
- Features: `Employed`, `Bank Balance`, `Annual Salary` (the `Index` column is dropped)
- Target: `Defaulted?` (1 = default, 0 = no default)
- Strong class imbalance: only about 3% of borrowers default
- Train/test split: 70% / 30%, stratified so both sets keep the same default rate

## Results

### Step 1: Baseline model (Logistic Regression, no class weighting)

| Metric | Value |
|---|---|
| Accuracy | 97.3% |
| Precision (defaulters) | 0.69 |
| Recall (defaulters) | 0.27 |
| F1-score (defaulters) | 0.38 |

|  | Predicted: No default | Predicted: Default |
|---|---|---|
| **Actual: No default** | 2,895 | 11 |
| **Actual: Default** | 69 | 25 |

A model that predicts "no default" for everyone would score 96.9% on this test set, so 97.3% accuracy is barely better than guessing. The baseline model catches only 27% of real defaulters.

